NDONTWRITEBYTECODE=1, no __pycache__); crash/idempotency evidence for every durable write (landed no-replace + fsync + hooks + real-kill pattern) or an explicit purity bound; conformance matrix mapping each pinned clause to a test or a stated bound; README package section with test commands and NO capability/CLI claim (no ax command exists); LOGBOOK entry newest-first. Managed Story worktree contract: the Story worktree .temp/STORY-260830-1cyj0q/worktree on branch task-board/story/STORY-260830-1cyj0q ALREADY EXISTS and its tip is your predecessor leaf's signed checkpoint commit — do not re-provision it, do not rebase it, do not commit on it, do not amend or revert the checkpoint, and never edit .task-board directly or self-accept. Your candidate is the UNCOMMITTED working tree on top of that checkpoint: exactly one leaf's worth of delta. Before you start, read the predecessor's landed delta (`git -C .temp/STORY-260830-1cyj0q/worktree show --stat HEAD`) and its outcome resources so you extend it instead of restating it. If origin/main moves before your handoff run `task-board worktree refresh-candidate TASK-260830-2g5be6` (LOGBOOK append conflicts are resolved with --replay-resolutions: copy the retained resolution-template.json header and add {\"checkpoint_oid\": <the ORIGINAL checkpoint OID recorded before the refresh>, \"path\": \"LOGBOOK.md\", \"sha256\": <hex of the full replacement>, \"content\": <base64>} keeping every entry newest-first), then audit every path in `git diff --name-only <old-base> <new-base>` that also appears in your `git status` and restore trunk content by hand — a refresh carries stale pre-refresh copies of trunk-changed files into the candidate and they read as reverts (README/LOGBOOK stay purely additive; task-board.config.json must equal HEAD byte-for-byte). Before handoff `git status` shows only candidate paths. Read the Curator-managed go-testing-tools skill first; all CI local; run the full configured validation suite (30 commands as of trunk c3aae73 — STORY-260830-4qojoz added three bounded rpcwire fuzz gates; read the count from the candidate config rather than assuming it) once before handoff (command 5 is `go test ./... -race -count=1 -timeout 25m` and can take 25-40 minutes under concurrent runs — that is expected, do not shorten it; command 21 is `cataloggen -adopted … -check`). Hand off no later than 3.5 hours in — if the full scope cannot be finished, hand off a reviewable slice with the remaining items enumerated precisely in the results. Attach TASK-260830-2g5be6_results.md, TASK-260830-2g5be6_conformance-matrix.md and TASK-260830-2g5be6_producer-evidence.tar.gz (a real gzip archive), keep the checklist current, then `task-board handoff TASK-260830-2g5be6 --role developer` and exit promptly. Canonical CLI /Users/iv/.curator/global/bin/task-board. Model: muse-spark max. The independent reviewer will be claude-opus-5 max.\n11|\n12|REJECTION PATTERNS THE INDEPENDENT REVIEWER HAS ALREADY CAUGHT ON THIS EPIC (2026-09-17: TASK-260830-1geqhj rev1, TASK-260830-24z2b3 rev2, TASK-260830-219okr rev3 were all routed CHANGES REQUESTED, and between them these six shapes account for every finding). Read this as a checklist you run against your own work BEFORE handoff; each item names what a reviewer will do to your candidate.\n13|(a) Circular expectations. An assertion that compares a production value against a production constant (`got.Code == pkg.SomeUnsupportedCode`) pins nothing: swapping the constant to another registered value with the same exit keeps the suite green. Assert the LITERAL token from the pinned specification text, at the production entry, and make sure the literal actually occurs in a committed test — not only in a comment. Derive every expectation from the spec, never from the implementation.\n14|(b) A rule pinned at the helper instead of the entry. A test that calls the inner helper directly proves the helper; it says nothing about whether the composed production entry still reaches it. Drive every rule through the entry point the conformance matrix names as its call site, and if a rule is implemented TWICE (a Build path and a Decode path, an exported entry and its internal sibling), each side needs its own narrowing mutant — a rule pinned only on one side leaves the other side unmeasured, and reviewers plant exactly there.\n15|(c) Forking a landed gate. Never re-implement what internal/environ, internal/canonicaljson, internal/scalar or another landed owner already gates (string bounds in CHARACTERS not bytes, uint53, digests, UUIDv7, timestamps, sorted-unique arrays, closed-shape decoding, the AX number model that forbids floating point, values at or beyond 2^53 and rounding-and-continuing). Delegate to it and keep only your package-specific rules. Every fork this epic produced became a defect: a byte-measured bound, an extension value rounded at 2^60, nested duplicate members collapsed by a lenient decode.\n16|(d) A census or an inventory standing in for behaviour. A reflection census of field NAMES does not prove that a cached observation cannot authorize; an arm inventory keyed by message text does not prove a condition edit is caught. Drive the input, assert the effect.\n17|(e) Mutant rows that do not measure what they claim. An arm-delete (`&& false`) is not a narrowing; a row patching two sites whose kill comes from one is one measured site reported as two; a plant with no raw log and no subprocess exit is a verdict line, not evidence. Ship one raw log per plant per run, state narrowing vs arm-delete per row, and include an applied harmless SURVIVED control so the harness is proven able to report a survivor.\n18|(f) Prose that outruns the tests. \"Composes X\", \"never admits what decoding refuses\", \"nothing re-implemented\", \"no cached sentinel can authorize\" are censusable claims: a reviewer greps for the callers and plants the counter-example. Before you write a sentence like that in a doc comment, README, LOGBOOK or TRACEABILITY row, make a test fail without the property. If you cannot, state it as an explicit BOUND instead, naming what is not covered.\n19|Finally: report the coverage ratio you MEASURED, not the one you planned. Two of the three rejected revisions claimed a complete ratio and the reviewer measured a smaller one; an honest smaller number with named gaps is accepted, an inflated one is a finding.\n20|\n21|TRUNK STATE AT YOUR SPAWN (recorded 2026-09-18): origin/main is c3aae73 as of 2026-09-18T01:30Z and carries BOTH the v0.7.0 adoption (PR #52, commit 2fc6d50) and the mesh-RPC Story plus the resumesmoke deadline fix (PR #53 a12d1bd, PR #54 c3aae73) — internal/specdoc/SPEC.v0.7.0.md, internal/specpin/v0.7.0.lock.json, internal/catalog/catalog.v0.7.0.json with `catalog.Adopted = ReleaseV070`, and internal/traceability/ownership.v0.7.0.json. Your Story branch was cut before that. You are a MIDDLE leaf, so you do NOT touch internal/traceability at all (the final leaf TASK-260830-32ypr2 carries the registry bindings and the pin) — but if origin/main moves before your handoff, run `task-board worktree refresh-candidate TASK-260830-2g5be6` and then audit every path in `git diff --name-only <old base> <new base>` that also appears in your `git status`: README.md and LOGBOOK.md moved on trunk with that landing, so keep your edits purely additive on top of the NEW content and never restore the old bytes. Once the refresh lands, cite SPEC.v0.7.0.md with the same heading numbers as the v0.6.0 sections named above.\n22|\n23|INHERITED ADVISORIES from the independent review that ACCEPTED the predecessor leaf (RUN-260918-3180f5 accepted TASK-260830-24z2b3 CR rev6 after closing an admit-hole where `encoding/json` rewrote invalid UTF-8 to U+FFFD and sealed it). It recorded three P3 measurement gaps \"for the sibling leaves\" — production is correct in each, but one member of a pinned class is unmeasured, which is precisely how this Story's earlier rounds went wrong twice. Read them in TASK-260830-24z2b3_review-verdict-rev6.md, close the ones your scope touches, and say in your results which you closed and which you deliberately left to the final leaf TASK-260830-32ypr2:\n24|- **P3-ε `hasUnknownClass` is pinned only for the INCLUDED unknown member.** The narrowing `if item.Class == \"unknown\" && item.Included()` (`capturebuild.go:418`, `R6-unknown-ignores-excluded`) **SURVIVED the whole package suite 2/2**. Under it, driven through the production entries (`logs/probe-under-mutant-unknown-excluded.log`): `BuildCaptureManifest` seals `raw_complete=true` for a manifest carrying an excluded unknown-class item (spec: \"Unknown makes raw completeness false\"), `DecodeCaptureManifest` admits `raw_complete=true` beside that item, and `RefuseMaximalSafeUnlessComplete` no long\n25|- **P3-ζ the lone-surrogate member is unpinned at the shared text gate.** `R6-text-helper-lone-title` (`validText` admits exactly one WTF-8 lone-surrogate title) **SURVIVED 2/2**: the only committed lone-surrogate row (`capture_source_identity_native_lone_surrogate`) reaches `SanitizeNativeKey`'s own `utf8.ValidString`, never `validText`. §1.6 names lone surrogates explicitly and the rev4 fix scope named them; production refuses them at every site (section T, `lone` rows). Fix: one lone-surrogate row through a `validText` site (e.g. the title) plus a harness row.\n26|- **P3-η `EncodeNativeIdentity` standalone admits identity fields decoding refuses.** Section T5: a 513-character native session ID or opaque identity, kind `bogus`, and a zero `LogicalWorkspaceID` (sealed as `\"\"`) are all encoded. Both production callers (`IdentityDigest`, `buildCaptureManifest`) re-decode and refuse (T5-2, T5-3), and the doc comment says \"renders one validated identity\", so no production path seals them; but the exported entry now advertises \"encoding never admits what decoding refused\" for extensions and native keys while still trusting the caller for bounds, vocabulary and","correlation_facts":{"tool_name":"read_file","outcome":"success"}}}
